    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d616">

                <pb facs="0754" n="744" sameAs="frus:frus1948v07/pg744"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00/9–748: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Stuart</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Nanking</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1948-09-07T18:00:00+09:00">September 7,
                            1948—6 p. m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received September 8—4:26 a. m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1636. In making decision on Embtel 1571, August 25, regarding Chinese suggestion
                    that Service Attaché’s aircraft be nominally placed under AAG, Department may
                    wish to consider following.</p>
                <pb facs="0755" n="745" xml:id="pg745"/>
                <p>Foreign Minister has taken firm decision that foreign diplomatic mission aircraft
                    would not at this stage of affairs be permitted to fly in China. In harmony with
                    this decision, he has flatly refused British Embassy permission fly its aircraft
                    in China, which it had sought to bring here from Hong Kong. In addition,
                    circular is going forward to all Missions in very near future advising of this
                    decision Chinese Government.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Stuart</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
